  about: [
    `<em>The Social Contract</em> is the book that made the French Revolution possible — a compact hundred pages, divided into four books of short chapters, written in prose so concentrated that almost every sentence has been quoted somewhere. Rousseau's opening is the most famous in political philosophy: "Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains." His question is not how this happened but what could make it legitimate. His answer — the general will, sovereignty as inalienable, the social compact — drove democratic revolutions and authoritarian movements with equal force for two centuries.`,
    `What the book actually argues is more careful, and more troubling, than its reputation. Rousseau does not defend every government in the name of the people; he sets a standard that nearly every actual government fails. The sovereign is the people themselves — not their representatives, not their king — and the sovereign cannot be transferred, divided, or delegated. The English, he says, think themselves free because they vote every few years; they are slaves from the moment the election is over. The argument is a permanent challenge to every actually existing democracy. Read it to understand where the idea of popular sovereignty comes from, and what it costs to take it seriously.`,
  ],
  chaptersSubtitle: 'All 48 chapters, by book and argument — from the opening chain to the closing religion.',
  chaptersLead: `<p>The Social Contract divides into four books of unequal weight. Book I (nine chapters) is the foundational argument: why force creates no right, what the social compact is, and what the sovereign is. Book II (twelve chapters) is the conceptual core: sovereignty, the general will, the law, and the figure of the Legislator. Book III (eighteen chapters) is the political theory proper: government as distinct from sovereignty, the forms of government, and how states die. Book IV (nine chapters) is the most practical, treating voting, elections, the Roman comitia, the dictatorship, and closing with the famous and troubling chapter on civil religion. Read it straight through — the argument is cumulative and the rhetoric tightens book by book.</p>`,
  themesByline: 'Five threads through the argument',
  themesLead: `The Social Contract is the book in which the modern idea of popular sovereignty receives its most rigorous and most disturbing formulation. Its five main threads are not independent; they form a single argument that Rousseau tightens across four books.`,

